ents.csv && wc -l events.csv && ls .claude/skills/zz-timezone-audit-20274"], ["Bash", "cat .claude/skills/zz-timezone-audit-20274/SKILL.md; echo ---; cat events.csv"]], "final_message": "Yes, the timestamps in `events.csv` use mixed offsets, and one row has no offset at all.\n\n| id | ts | Classification |\n|----|----|----------------|\n| 1 | `2026-03-01T10:00:00Z` | UTC (`Z`) |\n| 2 | `2026-03-01T10:05:00` | **No offset (naive)** |\n| 3 | `2026-03-01T04:10:00-06:00` | Explicit offset, UTC\u22126 (equals 10:10:00Z) |\n\n**Rows with no offset:** id 2 (`2026-03-01T10:05:00`).\n\n**Why the charts jump:**\n- **Naive row (id 2):** Parsers disagree on what to do with a timestamp that has no offset. Many treat it as local time, so it shifts by your machine's UTC offset. Others treat it as UTC. The result depends on where the chart code runs.\n- **Mixed offsets:** If the loader drops the offset and keeps the wall-clock time, id 3 lands at 04:10 instead of 10:10. That puts it 6 hours off from the other two.\n\nThe three events are probably about five minutes apart in real time (10:00Z, 10:05, 10:10Z). Ids 1 and 3 are consistent once normalized to UTC. Id 2 is the ambiguous one.\n\n**Fix:**\n1. Find out what the source system meant for the naive rows. If it's UTC, append `Z`. If it's a local zone, attach that zone explicitly.\n2. Parse everything with an offset-aware parser and convert to UTC before bucketing, for example `pd.to_datetime(df.ts, utc=True)`.\n3. Make the loader fail or warn on naive timestamps, so they can't slip in unnoticed.\n\nI haven't found the chart-building code in this repo yet. If you point me to it, I can check how it parses `ts`. This sample has only 3 rows, so I can't tell how many naive rows the full dataset has.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}